      "program_name": "Project-Based Vouchers and RAD conversions, Fresno Housing (Housing Authority of the County of Fresno CA028)",
      "administering_agency": "Fresno Housing (Housing Authorities of the City and County of Fresno); PBV program contact Aurora Ibarra (559) 443-8459, real estate Michael Duarte (559) 443-8492",
      "funding_type": "rental assistance",
      "typical_award_size": "The 2027 County Annual Plan lists PBV and RAD assistance proposed for 2025-2026 at County public housing sites: Pinedale I and II (57 units), DeSoto Gardens (40), Marcelli Terrace (24), Del Rey Complex (30), Laton Apartments (20), Sunset Terrace I (20), Mendoza Terrace (44) and II (29), Firebaugh Elderly (30), Cardella Courts (32), Helsem Terrace (40), Biola Apartments (12), Taylor Terrace (27), San Joaquin Apartments (20) and Cazares Terrace I (24). Pipeline with PBVs: Firebaugh La Joya Commons Phase II (40 units, up to 39 PBVs), Orange Cove Senior Housing (60 units, up to 50 PBVs or RAD transfer), Caruthers Commons (60), Kingsburg (80), Selma (80), Del Rey Family Housing (up to 127 multifamily and 45 single-family), Cordillera Commons Phase I, San Joaquin (50), Firebaugh RAD (152). Past competitive awards to third parties ran 15 to 50 PBVs per project.",
      "per_unit_cap": "Not applicable; HAP contracts up to 20 years with extensions.",
      "affordability_requirement": "PBV households generally at 30% to 40% AMI selected through Fresno Housing waiting lists and Coordinated Entry for PSH; RAD conversions preserve public housing affordability.",
      "eligible_project_types": [
        "new construction",
        "supportive housing",
        "senior housing",
        "farmworker housing"
      ],
      "application_cycle": "rolling",
      "next_deadline": "Fresno Housing attaches PBVs to its own and affiliate-owned projects without competition (Administrative Plan exception for PHA-owned units, typically with $40,000 per unit minimum hard costs); third-party owners must respond to a published RFP advertised in newspapers and on fresnohousing.org for three consecutive weeks. No RFP was open on fresnohousing.org this pass; past public notices (Avalon Commons, Crossroads Village, Sun Lodge, Step Up at 99) show awards of 15 to 50 PBVs. Watch the Boards of Commissioners meeting books for RFP authorizations.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "unclear",
      "layering_notes": "Fresno County splits housing work three ways: the Department of Public Works and Planning, Community Development Division (ComDev) runs the Urban County HUD programs, PLHA and the annual Affordable Housing Development NOFO; the County Administrative Office's new Office of Housing and Homelessness (OHH, created November 18, 2025) administers HHAP, the Continuum of Care and Transitional Rent and is absorbing housing programs from Behavioral Health and ComDev (Phase 2 approved November 3, 2026); and the Department of Behavioral Health co-applies for Homekey+ and NPLH and funds BHSA housing. Fresno Housing (the joint City and County Housing Authorities) is the dominant developer and voucher source. The City of Fresno and Clovis are separate entitlements with their own programs (Pass 3). fresnocountyca.gov and fresnohousing.org open normally; Board records are on fresnocounty.legistar.com and the Legistar web API client 'fresnocounty'. Fresno Housing is both the PHA and the county's most prolific developer (over 2,100 units since 2010, $600 million of development), so most County PBVs flow to its own tax credit and RAD deals; a third-party developer's realistic path is a joint venture with Fresno Housing as co-general partner, as at Sendero Commons' sister project Davu Village, or a competitive RFP when one is published. Davis-Bacon applies to PBV new construction of nine or more units. Source is the 2027 Annual Plan for the Housing Authority of the County of Fresno (draft) on fresnohousing.org; selection rules from the 2027 County Administrative Plan chapter 17.",
      "source_url": "https://fresnohousing.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Attachement-A2-2027-Annual-Plan-Housing-Authority-of-the-County-of-Fresno-Draft.pdf",
